TOOLIFYING THE UNCENSORED GENERATION ROUTE
==========================================
Time : 2026-10-21 06:57

QUESTION
  Should the Qwen-Image route become a tool that other conversations can call, rather than a command line
  the user has to run by hand?

ANSWER
  Yes, and it is done. The tool is registered and verified end to end.

IMPLEMENTATION
  Added `image_generate_uncensored` to the existing vision MCP server (D:\AI_file\mcp\vision-server.py),
  now v1.12.0, so no new MCP entry, no new configuration, and no new process are needed. Every
  conversation that already sees mcp__vision__* tools sees this one too.

  It shells out to sd-cli.exe (the engine has no daemon mode). Each call therefore re-reads about 9.3 GB
  of weights: roughly 4 s with a warm file cache, closer to a minute cold. That cost is accepted rather
  than solved, because building a resident server would mean writing and maintaining a second service.

KEY DESIGN DECISIONS
  1. A SEPARATE TOOL, not a flag on image_generate.
     The two models carry different licences - FLUX is Apache-2.0, these weights are Qwen Research
     License (research/evaluation only, no commercial use). Folding them behind one tool name would make
     it possible to produce a commercially unusable image while believing the licence was Apache-2.0.
     Separate names make the distinction impossible to miss.
  2. FLUX STAYS THE DEFAULT.
     Qwen-Image is about 14x slower for a comparable job, so it is reserved for the case the user
     actually asks for: uncensored output or a different model.
  3. HARD CONSTRAINTS ENFORCED IN CODE, not in prose.
     steps < 20            -> ValueError (4 steps was measured and is unusable blurry noise)
     width/height % 32 != 0 -> ValueError (the sampler requires it)
     width/height > 1024    -> ValueError (keeps the call inside the MCP timeout)
  4. THE LICENCE WARNING TRAVELS WITH THE TOOL.
     It appears in the tool description (so any model calling it reads the restriction), in the return
     payload, and in the rules. A restriction that lives only in documentation gets skipped.

MEASURED END TO END (through the MCP tool, not just the function)
  prompt  : a steam locomotive on a mountain bridge, wide shot
  512x512, 20 steps, cfg 6.0, euler  ->  84.0 s, returned path + licence line
  A second call via plain Python (bypassing MCP) took 89.3 s, and a 4-step call rendered unusable noise,
  confirming the minimum-steps guard is justified rather than arbitrary.

MCP TIMEOUT RAISED
  toolCallTimeoutMs in ~/.dsh/profiles/desktop/cordis.patch.yml went from 300000 to 600000, because a
  1024x1024 render needs 5-6 minutes and would otherwise be killed mid-flight. The previous file was
  backed up alongside it.

